## Iterate through the remove list
Here is where I use an iterative and conditional statement so that the ip_addresses removes any ip addresses that contain or are in the remove_list. When using a ‘for i’ or in this example ‘for element’ statement, I use this to check for values within the list, which is the ip_addresses variable. The conditional statement I use is ‘if element in remove_list’ which is where the function looks through and sees if the ‘element’ that the function is searching for is also within the remove_list. Once the function searching through the ip_addresses finds that the ‘element’ is also in the remove_list, I use the function ‘ip_addresses.remove(element)’ to remove any ip addresses that are in the remove_list section. 

Input: 
import_file = "allow_list.txt"

remove_list = ["192.168.97.225", "192.168.158.170", "192.168.201.40", "192.168.58.57"]

with open(import_file, "r") as file:
    ip_addresses = file.read()

ip_addresses = ip_addresses.split()

for element in ip_addresses:
    if element in remove_list:
        ip_addresses.remove(element)

print(ip_addresses)

## Remove IP addresses that are on the remove list
Based on the list above, where the code provides an interactive and specifically a conditional statement that removes the ip addresses within the remove list, this allows the print(ip_addresses) to display a list of ip_addresses that are specifically within the allow list and will not display any ip addresses in the remove_list section. 

